<center><TABLE BORDER=0 CELLPADDING=3 CELLSPACING=3 width=95%><tr><td width=100%><P><p><i><b><font size = 4>The Sims Online</b></i></font><br><BLOCKQUOTE><p align=justify>So there I was, playing The Sims Online, a new MMORPG from Electronic Arts, when someone asks, "Is anyone here from EverQuest?"  There was a second of silence.  "59 wizard here, from Drinal"  and then another spoke, "52 Bard from Bertoxxulous"  and another, "54 Druid from Veeshan here."  This was all simply amazing.<br><br>

What makes MMORPGs so fascinating?  Is it all about the game content and cool graphics and battles?  Maybe it's the realtime virtual worlds, and the unexpected changes which come from gaming online and interacting with other humans.  Maybe there are two types of gamers.  The social gamer, and the power gamer, but lets just talk about the social gamer for a moment. <br><br>

The Sims Online (TSO) is everything a social gamer wants in an MMORPG, without the killing and extreme need for time commitment.  I say "social gamer" because if racking up kills and points and weilding a sword is your forte, I'm fairly certain that you will get bored of TSO, very quickly.  If you like interacting with other humans from the comfort of your computer chair, this might be the game for you. <br><br>

That's right.  I say this is the game for the people who like to socialize.  It allows you to interact with others in a relaxed atmousphere.  Yeah, relaxed.  In TSO, you are allowed to step away from the keyboard for a few minutes.  So go ahead, tuck your kids into bed, hug your spouse, let the dog outside, or put dinner on the table.  Don't worry.  It will not result in a bloody massacre and 4 hour corpse retrieval.  Did you want to get a cool object for your sim?  Then go get it.  Don't waste your night staring at a screen; ignoring your family while waiting for some pixels to appear out of nowhere.  Sound good?  Then read on!<br><br>

<b>Starting Out:  </b>  Like most MMORPGs when starting out, you get to select a server which you would consider as a virtual city.  You then get to create a "sim" character at the start up.  Your sim starts out in the game with money.  Exactly ten thousand "simolians" (isn't that cute?) with which they can buy their own vacant lot, or you can have your sim wander around and get to know people before perhaps settling down with some roomates.  <br><br>

<b>Exploring Properties: </b>    Don't be shy now.  This is the kind of game where people want you to visit their properties and spend time on them, provided you are a polite guest.  So, eat their food, sleep in their beds, and wear their clothes, relax in their jacuzzi, and swim in their pool.  Dance, talk, and watch some of those hilarious sim interactions.  There are all kinds of different categories of properties.  There are "Welcome" properties which specialize in helping newbies and helping them get accustomed to the game.  There are also properties which specialize in helping you learn skills, or ones where you can gamble your simolians, and there's a different kind of property for just about anything.<br><br>
 
<b>Roomates:</b>  The more roomates a property has, the less it costs to do things like, upgrade the lot to a larger size.  The larger the size of the lot, the more you can build, and the more stuff you can have on it.   Some of this "stuff" makes it possible for your sim to make money.  <br><br>

<b> Skills & Making Money:</b>  Unlike regular Sims, your sim does not have a job.  Your sim can learn skills such as cooking or logic, or creativity or charisma or body.  Put your skills to the test.  Try and make money at "job objects" such as potion tables which require the logic skill, or painting which needs creativity, or telemarketing, which needs charisma.  The money earned from these job objects, is determined by the level of skill your sim may or may not have.  It is also determined by the number of people working on that object with you.  There are some job objects which require more than one person, with any number of skills.  These objects are more like interactive puzzle games where the players work cooperatively together to solve the puzzles.  If they succeed, they all get some money. <br><br>

<b>The Friendship Network:</b>  Another object of the game is to network and socialize.  The more friends that your sim makes, the more interactions or, emotes, your sim gets.  Each sim has page with a profile and a friendship web, which allows others to see who their friends and enemies are.<br><br>

<b> Cheating In This Game:</b>   As always in almost any MMORPG, there's a way to cheat the system and get rich quick.  You might ask, "But what's to prevent me from making another sim and transferring money?"  Here's what stops you:  You can only create one sim per server.  "But what's to prevent you from cross server trading?"  Here's what stops you:  That used to occur a lot.  Recently, Electronic Arts implemented a patch, and now after you create a sim, it will be a period of seven days before you are able to delete it.  Sure, you can still do cross server trading, but you won't be making money much faster than anyone else.<br><br>

<b>Scamming:</b>   Due to the anonymity of the internet and the fact that some people are just born jerks, no MMORPG is 100% chock-full of trustworthy and honest players.  If you're lucky, maybe 90% are.  Watch out for the other 10%.  Here are some things of note:  Your roomates will have the ability to make changes to the construction of your property.  Be very careful when choosing a roomate, lest you end up losing your brilliant property design to someone who's feeling destructive.  Likewise, be careful when moving in with someone.  Never have your sim pay money to move in with someone.  Buying objects for the property is okay, because you can keep your objects when you move out.  <br><br>


<b> Control Of Your Property:</b>  Imagine being given the tools to design the look of your very own zone.... and being allowed to boot out players who are disruptive, or invite friends to spend time on it and enjoy it.  Do you want a property that looks like a miniature Venice?  How about a city, or a park, a library, or a theatre or a mall or a mansion?  You're in YOUR world, now.<br><br>

<b> General Game Bugginess: </b>   Must of their bugs, they seem to fix in the next patch.  My personal favorite was the one where players were making $1k per pizza each, on a group object.  Since your success in the game largely depends upon you and your personality, I don't think this threw off the game economy too badly.  There are still some issues with invisible plates, tables, and objects, and sometimes players will freeze at buffet tables.. but these are mild, and last only a couple of minutes.  ...And since your sim isn't going to die, it's really not a big deal.<br><br>

Okay, now that you know some of the ins and outs, and ups and downs of the game...  I'd like to share some of my experiences about the game with you.  So far, it's been incredibly fun and social.  I have found the majority of the players to be in the 25+ age range.  This means there are more people who type in real sentences and the bouts of d00dspeak are few and far between.  There seems to be a fairly even mix of both men and women.   Meanwhile, there is no killing, looting, exp grinding, time sinks, drama nor arguing about the spoils of loot which occurs in EverQuest.     I would like to thank the makers of TSO, for curing me of my EverQuest addiction.  Granted, I 'm about to have a TSO addiction, but we'll cross that bridge when we get there.  So, if you happen to buy this game, please log onto Jolly Pines and say "Hi" to Ellie Mae.
<br><br>
I'm giving it a 9.  
<br><br>
Thanks for reading.
